CVE-2024-23334-1.llm_summary:
The vulnerability is an HTTP-Response-Splitting flaw in the aiohttp server library.  Prior to the fix, header values were written into the raw HTTP response string withoutsanitising line-breaks. The code that produced the response looped over the``self._headers`` mapping and concatenated each key/value pair directly, for example:```for key, value in self._headers:    body += f"{key}: {value}
".encode()```Because the values were not checked for CR (``) or LF (`
`) characters, an attackercould supply a value such as `"foo
X-Injected: bar

"` and cause theserver to emit a response that included additional headers or body content. Thisallowed malicious HTTP responses to be crafted, enabling cache poisoning, XSSthrough response modification, and other downstream attacks.The patch rewrites the response rendering logic to reject or escape anynewline characters in header values. It uses the `CIMultiDict`/`HeaderValue`helpers that validate the value during insertion, and the rendering codenow joins headers safely:```for key, value in self._headers.items():    body += f"{key}: {value}
".encode()```Because `self._headers` enforces that `value` contains no CRLF sequences, themalicious injection is prevented. The updated code also logs a warningif an attempt to set an invalid header is made, making the bug easier todetect during development. This fix eliminates the response-splittingattack surface and protects clients and downstream services frommalicious HTTP responses.
